course: dictionary
lesson: 11994
title: Chaotic: when nothing is in order
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Chaotic means in a state of complete confusion and disorder, with no plan or control. «The morning rush was chaotic: shoes everywhere, toast burning, and nobody could find their bag.» The clues shoes everywhere, burning and nobody could find show there is no order at all.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. «The morning rush was muddled, with no plan or control» still makes sense, so muddled and disorderly is a good match for chaotic.
explain: Chaotic is also a science word. In science, a chaotic system is one where tiny changes at the start lead to huge, unpredictable differences later, like the weather. That is why forecasts for more than a week or two are hard to trust.
fact: Chaotic means in a state of complete confusion and disorder, with no plan or control.
fact: Chaotic is an adjective: chaotic, more chaotic, most chaotic.
fact: «The classroom was chaotic after the bell rang» shows a place where everyone is rushing and nothing is in order.
fact: In Alice, the Mad Hatter's tea party is chaotic because people keep changing seats and nothing follows a plan.
fact: In science, a chaotic system has tiny starting changes that lead to huge, unpredictable differences later, like the weather.
exercise: «The morning rush was chaotic: shoes everywhere, toast burning, and nobody could find their bag.» What does chaotic mean? | Quiet and sleepy | Clean and tidy | Calm and peaceful | *Completely muddled, with no order
exercise: Which word means the opposite of chaotic? | *Orderly | Noisy | Messy | Wild
exercise: In science, a chaotic system is one where tiny changes at the start lead to huge differences later. Why are weather forecasts hard beyond a week or two? | The weather never changes at all | Forecasters are too lazy to check | Weather only happens in summer | *Tiny changes grow into big, unpredictable differences
test: «After the party, the living room was chaotic, with balloons, crumbs and toys everywhere.» What does chaotic mean here? | Neat and ready for guests | *Messy and in complete disorder | Very cold and empty | Full of quiet music
test: «The Mad Hatter's tea party is chaotic: everyone keeps changing seats.» What clue shows the party is chaotic? | The hatter is wearing a tall hat | The party is in a garden | *Everyone keeps changing seats | The tea is hot
exam: Which sentence uses chaotic correctly? | The chaotic sky was pale blue and calm | I ate a chaotic sandwich for lunch | The chaotic book sat quietly on the shelf | *The chaotic traffic jam made everyone late, with cars going every which way
